{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_011RizE2LYupyoyMRQfxpbGe","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"copper tracer self-diffusion activation energy D0 Arrhenius Rothman Peterson\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Stokes-Einstein relation and excess entropy scaling law in liquid Copper\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.07795\"},{\"title\":\"Molecular dynamics modeling of self-diffusion along a triple junction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1302.1962\"},{\"title\":\"zendy.io\",\"url\":\"https://zendy.io/title/10.1002/crat.19790141216\"},{\"title\":\"srd.nist.gov\",\"url\":\"https://srd.nist.gov/jpcrdreprint/1.3253129.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"elib.dlr.de\",\"url\":\"https://elib.dlr.de/62936/\"},{\"title\":\"informahealthcare.com\",\"url\":\"https://informahealthcare.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01418617808239226\"},{\"title\":\"Institute of Metals Division - Effect of Orientation on the Surface Self-Diffusion of Copper\",\"url\":\"https://onemine.org/documents/institute-of-metals-division-effect-of-orientation-on-the-surface-self-diffusion-of-copper\"},{\"title\":\"link.springer.com\",\"url\":\"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF03038390\"},{\"title\":\"Extractive Metallurgy Division - Self-Diffusion of Copper in Molten Copper\",\"url\":\"https://onemine.org/documents/extractive-metallurgy-division-self-diffusion-of-copper-in-molten-copper\"},{\"title\":\"springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com\",\"url\":\"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/doi/10.1007/BF02642463\"}]\n\nThe search didn't turn up the Rothman or Peterson papers, so I have no source for their values. The results did include several copper self-diffusion measurements:\n\n- **Krautheim et al. (1979), ⁶⁴Cu tracer in Cu, 1078–1348 K:** The authors report D₀ of about 0.68 cm²/s (with asymmetric uncertainties) and Q₀ of 2.17 ± 0.02 eV. They also observe a slight curvature in the Arrhenius plot, which they attribute to a divacancy contribution for the frequency factor D 0 and the activation energy Q 0 we found D 0 = (0.68 −0.12 +0.16 ) cm 2 · s −1 and Q 0 = (2.17 ± 0.02) eV. A slight bending of the Arrhenius plot suggests a divacancy contribution.\n- **Separate Cu-tracer study (1980s, abstract only):** Fitting the curved plot with earlier tracer data gives monovacancy and divacancy activation energies of about 47.4 and 57 kcal/mol, respectively (Q 1V = 47·4 ± 0·6 and Q 2V = 57 ± 3 kcal mole−1).\n- **Butrymowicz, Manning & Read (NBS, 1973):** A critical review of copper volume and surface self-diffusion data, which the authors describe as covering the topic in detail; I did not see its recommended D₀ or Q values in the results.\n- **Molecular dynamics (arXiv 1302.1962):** This paper tabulates Arrhenius Q and D₀ for lattice self-diffusion in Cu with an embedded-atom potential, but the numerical table did not come through in the snippet.\n- **Liquid Cu (arXiv 1512.07795):** Ab initio MD gives an activation energy of about 370 meV (LDA) and 475 meV (GGA), with experiment cited as about 337 meV.\n\nThe Choi & Shewmon surface self-diffusion abstract reports Q ≈ 49 kcal/mol and D₀ on the order of 10⁻⁴ cm²/s, but the exponent is garbled in the source text, so verify it before relying on it.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"3ae5c95b-c115-4692-9fff-e871f3e472c9","uuid":"d7d87572-856b-47ad-96bd-559547c3ce3e","timestamp":"2026-11-01T03:58:05.229Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"copper tracer self-diffusion activation energy D0 Arrhenius Rothman Peterson","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_01UijwY75KdsH3nrGa7qgn4f","content":[{"title":"Stokes-Einstein relation and excess entropy scaling law in liquid Copper","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1512.07795"},{"title":"Molecular dynamics modeling of self-diffusion along a triple junction","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1302.1962"},{"title":"zendy.io","url":"https://zendy.io/title/10.1002/crat.19790141216"},{"title":"srd.nist.gov","url":"https://srd.nist.gov/jpcrdreprint/1.3253129.pdf"},{"title":"elib.dlr.de","url":"https://elib.dlr.de/62936/"},{"title":"informahealthcare.com","url":"https://informahealthcare.com/doi/abs/10.1080/01418617808239226"},{"title":"Institute of Metals Division - Effect of Orientation on the Surface Self-Diffusion of Copper","url":"https://onemine.org/documents/institute-of-metals-division-effect-of-orientation-on-the-surface-self-diffusion-of-copper"},{"title":"link.springer.com","url":"https://link.springer.com/article/10.1007/BF03038390"},{"title":"Extractive Metallurgy Division - Self-Diffusion of Copper in Molten Copper","url":"https://onemine.org/documents/extractive-metallurgy-division-self-diffusion-of-copper-in-molten-copper"},{"title":"springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com","url":"https://springerlink.bibliotecabuap.elogim.com/doi/10.1007/BF02642463"}]},"The search didn't turn up the Rothman or Peterson papers, so I have no source for their values. The results did include several copper self-diffusion measurements:\n\n- **Krautheim et al. (1979), ⁶⁴Cu tracer in Cu, 1078–1348 K:** The authors report D₀ of about 0.68 cm²/s (with asymmetric uncertainties) and Q₀ of 2.17 ± 0.02 eV. They also observe a slight curvature in the Arrhenius plot, which they attribute to a divacancy contribution for the frequency factor D 0 and the activation energy Q 0 we found D 0 = (0.68 −0.12 +0.16 ) cm 2 · s −1 and Q 0 = (2.17 ± 0.02) eV. A slight bending of the Arrhenius plot suggests a divacancy contribution.\n- **Separate Cu-tracer study (1980s, abstract only):** Fitting the curved plot with earlier tracer data gives monovacancy and divacancy activation energies of about 47.4 and 57 kcal/mol, respectively (Q 1V = 47·4 ± 0·6 and Q 2V = 57 ± 3 kcal mole−1).\n- **Butrymowicz, Manning & Read (NBS, 1973):** A critical review of copper volume and surface self-diffusion data, which the authors describe as covering the topic in detail; I did not see its recommended D₀ or Q values in the results.\n- **Molecular dynamics (arXiv 1302.1962):** This paper tabulates Arrhenius Q and D₀ for lattice self-diffusion in Cu with an embedded-atom potential, but the numerical table did not come through in the snippet.\n- **Liquid Cu (arXiv 1512.07795):** Ab initio MD gives an activation energy of about 370 meV (LDA) and 475 meV (GGA), with experiment cited as about 337 meV.\n\nThe Choi & Shewmon surface self-diffusion abstract reports Q ≈ 49 kcal/mol and D₀ on the order of 10⁻⁴ cm²/s, but the exponent is garbled in the source text, so verify it before relying on it."],"durationSeconds":6.899180693999999,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_011RizE2LYupyoyMRQfxpbGe","permission_decision":{"decision":"accept","source":"config","reason_type":"mode"}}]}
